# The moment (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, phase 7): the weather, as two residents see it. Many fit nearly anyone (a topic
# and at most one fact), so any pair has a deep pool; the rest are the sharp, rare moments (snow at dawn on a market
# day, fog on the shore at night). Tagged answers set a mark so the reply fits them. Drafts, to tune by ear.

# ================================================================ Anyone, any sky

scene m7_wx_sky_reading
when topic=weather
a : My gran could read the sky like a ledger. I can't read it at all. | Gulls inland. That means more of this. | My tail itched all yesterday. Should've listened. | The cat washed behind her ears last night. I should've known.
b[old] #lore : Depends who you ask. My mother had a saying for every cloud, and half of them disagreed.
b[farmer] #lore : Signs are nothing. I've watched skies forty years. They lie like merchants.
b : Nobody can. It does what it likes. | Gulls go where the fish guts are. That's all. | Mine itches when I've fleas. | That cat washes every day.
a@lore : Then why do we all keep saying them?
a : You've no poetry in you. | Spoilsport. | Rude. | Well, one day I'll be right.

scene m7_wx_what_to_wear
when topic=weather
a : I never know what to wear this time of year. | Coat or no coat? I've changed my mind three times. | I came out in my thin cloak. Mistake. | Wore my good wool, and now it smells like a wet sheep.
b[wealth=rich] #rich : I've a servant who carries the other coat.
b[young] #pup : These boots were somebody else's first. They're huge.
b : Wear both and take one off. | Coat. Always the coat. | Learn from it. | Hang it by the fire tonight.
a@rich : Must be nice.
a@pup : You'll grow into them.
a : Wise. | I'll learn when I'm dead. | Next time. | And smell sheep all evening. Lovely.

scene m7_wx_almanac
when topic=weather
a : The old almanac says a hard winter. | Somebody at the inn swore it'd be a wet year. | Heard the weather's turning for good, this year. | Somebody's goose stood on one leg all day. Means something.
b : The almanac's never been right. | Somebody at the inn swears a lot of things. | They say that every year. | It means the goose was tired.
a : Once. It was right once. | True. He swore at me. | And one year they'll be right. | You're no fun.
b?[humour=dry] : I'll make a note. Wet year, hard winter, goose tired.

scene m7_wx_old_days
when topic=weather a.stage=adult|old
a : Summers were hotter when I was young. | Winters were whiter when I was a pup. | We never had weather like this. Never. | Seasons used to come when they were told.
b[old] #old : Everything was more, when we were young. Including us.
b : You were shorter. Everything looks bigger. | You say that every year. | You did. You just don't remember it. | They still don't listen to you.
a@old : Ha. True enough.
a : I'll grant you that. | And I'll keep saying it. | I remember everything. | Nobody does.

scene m7_wx_brief
when topic=weather
a : Weather. | Lovely day. | Grim day. | Day, at least.
b : Weather. | Is it? | Grim. | So it is.
a : That's settled, then. | No. | Grim. | Right.

scene m7_wx_wet_dog
when topic=weather here.weather=rain|storm
a : I smell like wet dog. | My fur's gone flat as a pond. | I've rain in my ears. Both of them. | My mother made us shake off in the yard. Like dogs.
b[young] #shake : Watch this! Brrrrr!
b : We all do, in this. | Mine's in ringlets. Don't laugh. | Tip your head. It runs out. | Mine still does.
a@shake : Oi! I was nearly dry!
a : Small comfort. | I'm laughing inside. | It's running in, not out. | Mothers know.

scene m7_wx_umbrella
when topic=weather here.weather=rain
a : Saw one of the fine folk with an umbrella. | Somebody's selling oiled hoods at the corner. | My hat's a bucket now. | I tried a sack over my head. Worked till it didn't.
b : An umbrella? In this wind? | Buy two. One for your tail. | Tip it out, then. | Fashion.
a : It went inside out. He went with it. | My tail's past saving. | Into my boot. | High fashion.

scene m7_wx_rain_sounds
when topic=weather here.weather=rain
a : I like the sound of it on the roof. Don't tell anyone. | That drip's been playing the same tune all day. | Rain on the shutters sends me straight to sleep. | Hear it running off everything? Like a river.
b[tone=gruff] #gruff : It's water falling. That's all.
b : Your secret's safe. | Same tune as yesterday. | Lucky. Keeps me awake. | It'll be in my cellar by dark.
a@gruff : You've a heart of slate.
a : Thank you. | It only knows the one. | Count the drips. | Bail fast.

scene m7_wx_rain_steaming
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.season=summer|spring
a : Warm rain. You can almost drink it. | Steam's coming off the road. | Smells like hot stone and green things. | Rain this warm feels like cheating.
b : I've stopped bothering with a hood. | Like a laundry. | Smells like the world's been washed. | Cheat away. I'll take it.
a : Me too. Freedom. | Without the soap. | And hung out to dry. | Cheers to it.

scene m7_wx_rain_cold
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.season=autumn|winter
a : Cold rain finds every gap in a coat. | My paws are ice to the wrist. | It's that rain that goes sideways. | Sleet, nearly. Mean stuff.
b[job=healer] #heal : Get dry tonight, properly dry. I've coughs enough.
b : Every gap, and some it makes. | Sit on them. Works for me. | Turn your back to it. | Nearly snow, nearly rain. Worst of both.
a@heal : Yes, healer.
a : Truly. | Rude, but true. | Then it goes round. | Worst of all.

scene m7_wx_snow_first_flake
when topic=weather here.weather=snow
a : Caught one on my tongue. Don't tell. | Look, each flake's different. Right on my sleeve. | The snow's squeaking under my boots. | Hush. Listen. You can hear it falling.
b[young] #pup : I've caught nine! Ten! Ten!
b : I caught three. We're even. | They melt before I can look. | Means it's proper cold. | I can't hear anything. Just my teeth.
a@pup : Champion of the snow.
a : Deal. | Quick, then. | Proper cold. | Then shut your mouth and listen.

scene m7_wx_snow_shovels
when topic=weather here.weather=snow here.time=dawn|day
a : Who's clearing the lane? | Took me an hour to dig out the door. | Somebody's built a snow watchman by the gate. Rude one. | The roofs are groaning.
b[job=laborer] #dig : I'll shovel anything, if they pay. They never pay.
b : Not me. | An hour? Mine's still shut. | Rude how? | Knock it off before it caves in.
a@dig : They'll pay in thanks.
a : Nor me. | Use the window. | I'll not say. Go and look. | With what? My tail?

scene m7_wx_snow_tracks
when topic=weather here.weather=snow
a : Fox tracks right across the yard. | Somebody's been round the back. Tracks to the door. | You can read the whole street's comings and goings in the snow. | Snow tells tales.
b[job=guard] #watch : Handy, that. Burglars hate snow.
b : Bold fox. | Let's hope it's a friend. | I know who went to the tavern. Twice. | Mine went straight home. Believe me.
a@watch : Do they get cold, burglars?
a : Hungry fox. | Let's hope. | Shh. | I believe you.

scene m7_wx_fog_voices
when topic=weather here.weather=fog
a : I can hear wolves talking but I can't see them. | Somebody just said my name. Was that you? | The #fog#'s got everyone whispering. | A bell somewhere. Can't tell where.
b[temper=nervy] #nervy : Don't. Please don't. I'm jumpy enough.
b : Odd, isn't it. Voices with no bodies. | Wasn't me. | Feels rude to shout. | Chapel bell. Or a cow.
a@nervy : Sorry. Sorry.
a : Ghosts with gossip. | Then who? | Like a chapel. | Or a ghost cow.

scene m7_wx_fog_lost
when topic=weather here.weather=fog
a : I walked past my own door. | I followed a cart into a yard and the cart went home. | I've walked the same lane twice now. | I'm navigating by bakery smell.
b : Count the doors. | Tie yourself to a fence. | It's the same lane. You're going round. | Best nose in town.
a : I can't see the doors to count. | Very funny. | I know. That's the trouble. | Second best. You've seen my belly.

scene m7_wx_fog_clearing
when topic=weather here.weather=fog here.time=day
a : It's lifting. Look, there's the roofline. | Sun's trying to burn through. | Half the town's come back out of the #fog#. | I can see the hill again.
b : There's the chapel. I was worried. | Go on, sun. | Wasn't missing much. | Hill looks surprised to be back.
a : Never left. | Go on! | Hush. | Ha.

scene m7_wx_storm_counting
when topic=weather here.weather=storm
a : One... two... three... | That flash lit up everything. | Thunder rolling round the hills like a barrel. | Lightning's walking closer.
b[young] #pup : Make it stop! Can you make it stop?
b : Three miles. Or three streets. I forget which. | Like day. Then darker. | Somebody's moving furniture up there. | Then let's walk away from it.
a@pup : It's only noise, little one. It's only noise.
a : Streets, I hope. | Darker than before. | Big furniture. | Faster than it.

scene m7_wx_storm_after
when topic=weather here.weather=storm
a : There goes somebody's washing. | That's a tile off the roof. | Hear the shutters? Bang, bang, bang. | Hold on to something.
b : Into the next street. | Not mine. Please not mine. | Nailed mine this morning. | I'm holding you.
a : Their shirts'll be in the next town by morning. | It's yours. | Smart. | I noticed.

scene m7_wx_clear_lazy
when topic=weather here.weather=clear here.time=dawn|day
a : Too nice to work. | Look at the cat. Flat out in the sun. | I could lie in this sun all day. | The sun's made everyone friendly.
b[working=yes] #work : Some of us don't get the choice.
b : Too nice not to. | Wise cat. | Do it. I'll guard you. | Even old Grumbles smiled.
a@work : I'll sun myself for you.
a : That's not how it works. | Wiser than us. | You'd sell me. | Mark the day.

scene m7_wx_clear_washing
when topic=weather here.weather=clear here.time=dawn|day
a : Washing day! Every line in town's full. | Sheets out, rugs out, pups out. | Good drying wind. | I've beaten the rug till it begged.
b : Mine went out at dawn. | Pups dry quickest. | Get the blankets out while it lasts. | It'll thank you.
a : Early bird. | And smell best. | All of them. | It won't.

scene m7_wx_overcast_mood
when topic=weather here.weather=overcast
a : Grey days make me sleepy. | Sky's the colour of old porridge. | Can't tell if it's morning or evening. | Not a shadow anywhere.
b : Everything does, you. | Cold porridge. | Neither can the cockerel. | Good. Shadows make me nervous.
a : Fair. | Lumpy, too. | He's been crowing all day. | Odd one.

scene m7_wx_overcast_waiting
when topic=weather here.weather=overcast
a : It's going to pour. Any moment. | Take your washing in. | Feels like a storm sitting on the roof. | Close, isn't it? Heavy air.
b[job=farmer] #farm : Let it. The ground's waiting.
b : It's been any moment since dawn. | Already did. | It's waiting for us to leave the house. | Like breathing soup.
a@farm : Not my washing.
a : Then it's due. | Smart. | Then stay in. | Lentil soup.

scene m7_wx_wind
when topic=weather here.weather=overcast|storm|clear here.season=autumn|winter|spring
a : The #wind#'s got teeth today. | Blew my hat clean off. | The weathercock's spinning like it's mad. | Hear it in the chimney? Moaning.
b : Keep your mouth shut, it gets in. | Where'd it land? | It is mad. Old bird. | That's my old aunt. She moans like that.
a : Mm-hm. | In the trough. | Madder by the day. | Ha! Don't let her hear.

# ================================================================ By the hour

scene m7_wx_dawn_birds
when topic=weather here.time=dawn here.weather=clear|overcast|fog
a : Birds are loud this morning. | Pink in the east. Might be a fair day. | Cold dawn. My breath's a cloud. | Dew on everything. My boots are soaked.
b[tired=yes] #tired : Don't. I've not been to bed yet.
b : Fighting over worms. | Pink and gold. Pretty. | Mine too. | Mine too. And the hem.
a@tired : I can tell.
a : Tell me about it. | Every day, and I forget. | Breathe at the cat. | Everything wet before work.

scene m7_wx_dusk_light
when topic=weather here.time=dusk here.weather=clear
a : Look at that sky. Orange as a fox. | Lamps coming on, one by one. | The bats are out. | Light's going early now.
b[old] #old : I've seen ten thousand of them. Still stop for every one.
b : Shepherd's delight. | That's the day done. | They'll have the moths. | It is. Mind the step.
a@old : So you should.
a : Shepherds can keep it. I'll look. | Done and dusted. | Good luck to them. | Ta.

scene m7_wx_night_stars
when topic=weather here.time=night here.weather=clear
a : Clear night. Frost on the cabbages by morning. | So many stars. You'd think someone spilled them. | The moon's so bright the owls are squinting. | #cold.capitalize# one. Can see my breath.
b : Cover them, then. | Spilled salt, my grandmother said. | Poor owls. | Can see everyone's.
a : Already did. | Throw some over your shoulder. | Poor mice, more like. | It's a breathing town.

scene m7_wx_night_rain
when topic=weather here.time=night here.weather=rain|storm
a : Bed's the only sensible place on a night like this. | Every lamp's gone out on the street. | Hear that? Somebody's roof is losing. | Wet and dark. Perfect.
b[job=guard] #watch : And here's me on watch in it till dawn.
b : Then why aren't we in it? | Leave them. Saves oil. | Not my problem till it's mine. | For burglars.
a@watch : I'll think of you. From my bed.
a : Good question. | Penny-pincher. | It'll be yours. | Exactly.

# ================================================================ By the place

scene m7_wx_gate_watch
when topic=weather here.place=gate
a : No travellers today. Not in this. | The road's empty both ways. | Saw a cart turn back at the bend. | Mud to the gate posts.
b[job=guard] #guard : Good. Fewer to search.
b : Nobody sensible. | Empty road, quiet gate. | Wise driver. | Somebody'll get stuck. Watch.
a@guard : You'd search the wind.
a : Then where are the fools? | Suits me. | Wiser than us. | Bet you a penny.

scene m7_wx_market_awning
when topic=weather here.place=market
a : The awnings are earning their keep today. | Stallholders all huddled under the cloth. | Everything on the stall's wet or warm. | Fewer buyers when it's like this.
b[role=merchant] #trade : I'd pay the weather to stay mild. Gladly.
b : Mine's leaking at the seam. | Like sheep in a fold. | Warm's worse. The cheese sweats. | More haggling, then.
a@trade : Who do you pay?
a : Tar it. | Baa. | Cheese has feelings too. | Worse haggling.

scene m7_wx_shore_sea
when topic=weather here.place=shore
a : The water's sulking today. | Look at the waves, white tops all over. | Calm as a pond. Never trust it. | Smell that wind off the water.
b[job=fisher|sailor] #sea : Mood's for wolves. Water just is. Respect it.
b : Like my aunt. | Some fool's out in a boat. | Calm water hides things. | Clears your head.
a@sea : I respect it. From here.
a : Leave it be, then. | Gods keep them. | That's what I said. | And your nose.

scene m7_wx_field_sky
when topic=weather here.place=field
a : Big sky out here. | You can see the weather coming from a mile off. | Wind across the field like a paw stroking fur. | Clouds racing their shadows.
b[job=farmer|herder] #farm : And I can tell you which bit will land on my crop.
b : Makes you feel small. | Can't hide from it, though. | Pretty. | Shadows are winning.
a@farm : Always your crop.
a : Small and windblown. | No. | It is. | Always do.

scene m7_wx_wild_road
when topic=weather here.place=wild
a : Long way to a roof from here. | Not a soul on the road. Just us and the sky. | The trees are talking in this #wind#. | You can smell the weather out here before it comes.
b : Walk faster, then. | Suits me. | Saying what? | I smell rain.
a : My legs are short. | Me too. | Hurry, probably. | Rain and pine.

scene m7_wx_tavern_door
when topic=day|smalltalk here.place=tavern here.season=autumn|winter
cooldown 1d
a : Shut the door! You're letting the weather in. | Every wolf who comes in brings a puddle. | Hear the wind out there? And us in here. | Fire's roaring. Let it do what it likes outside.
b : It came in on its own. | I'll mop with your tail. | Best sound in the world. | Cheers to the fire.
a : Then show it out. | You won't. | Second best. Best is ale pouring. | Cheers.

scene m7_wx_chapel_steps
when topic=day|faith here.place=chapel here.season=autumn|winter
cooldown 1d
a : Cold enough out there to make anyone pious. | Nice to be indoors for the sermon. | The chapel roof's dripping again. | Even the candles shiver today.
b[job=priest] #priest : The roof fund box is by the door. Just mentioning.
b : Warmer in here than at home, for some of us. | The sermon or the roof? | Into the font. Blessed rain. | So do I.
a@priest : Mentioned and heard.
a : That's why it's full. | Both. | Holy drips. | We'll all shiver together.

# ================================================================ By the season and its work

scene m7_wx_spring_mud
when topic=weather here.season=spring here.weather=rain|overcast
a : #mud.capitalize# season. | The lanes are soup. | I lost a boot in the yard. The yard kept it. | Thaw's turned everything to porridge.
b[job=farmer] #plough : Can't plough wet. We wait. Always wait.
b : Every year. Every single year. | Walk the stones. | Leave it, it's the yard's now. | Lumpy porridge.
a@plough : Waiting's half of farming.
a : Regular as Restday. | There aren't any. | It was a good boot. | And cold.

scene m7_wx_spring_blossom
when topic=weather here.season=spring here.weather=clear|overcast
a : Blossom on the hedges. | Lambs bleating everywhere. | Everything's waking up. | You can sit out after supper again.
b : Snow in spring, but sweeter. | And their mothers louder. | Not me. | In a coat.
a : Just as messy. | Like a market with legs. | You never wake. | In two.

scene m7_wx_summer_flies
when topic=weather here.season=summer here.weather=clear|overcast
a : The flies are thick as smoke. | Midges everywhere. They're eating me. | Bees in the lavender, loud as a mill. | Wasps at the jam again.
b : Swat them. | They like you. | Busy bees. | They like jam. Fair enough.
a : There are thousands. | They're welcome to me. | Busier than me. | They'll get a swat too.

scene m7_wx_summer_drought
when topic=weather here.season=summer here.weather=clear
a : The well's low. | Not a drop for three weeks. | The grass has gone the colour of straw. | The brook's showing its stones.
b[job=farmer] #farm : My beans are begging. I've been carrying buckets since dawn.
b : We'll be fighting for water soon. | Pray for rain. | Dry as a biscuit. | Saw a fish waving for help.
a@farm : I'll pray for your beans.
a : Hope not. | Every night. | A dry biscuit. | Ha. Poor fish.

scene m7_wx_autumn_leaves
when topic=weather here.season=autumn
a : The leaves are coming down like rain. | Everything smells of bonfires. | Conkers everywhere. The pups are at war. | Rooks building late. Mild winter, they say.
b[young] #pup : I've got a conker that's beaten twenty!
b : Pretty, though. | Somebody's burning something good. | Mine won. | Rooks say a lot of things.
a@pup : Twenty? Show me. Is it baked? That's cheating.
a : Messy, though. | Leaves, and somebody's old chair. | Course it did. | So do you.

scene m7_wx_autumn_harvest_race
when topic=weather here.season=autumn here.weather=overcast|rain
a : They're racing the rain in the fields. | Every hand in town's out cutting. | Last of the apples are falling. | They'll have to bring it in wet.
b[job=farmer|herder] #farm : Don't. Just don't. I've not slept.
b : Hope they win. | Except mine. | Wasps'll have them. | Wet grain rots.
a@farm : Go and sleep. I'll pray for dry.
a : Me too. | Lazy. | Not if I get there first. | Everyone knows.

scene m7_wx_winter_dark
when topic=weather here.season=winter
a : Dark by teatime. | I've not seen the sun all week. | The days are short as tempers. | I light the lamp at dawn and again at dusk.
b : Dark at breakfast too. | It's up there. Somewhere. | Shorter. | Oil's dear.
a : I'm living in a cellar. | Sulking. | Ha. Fair. | Everything's dear.

scene m7_wx_winter_ice
when topic=weather here.season=winter here.weather=clear|overcast|snow
a : The trough's iced over. The horses are sulking. | The milk froze in the jug overnight. | The pump's frozen. | Watch the steps. Glass, all of them.
b[old] #slip : I went down on the ice last winter. Still feel it in my hip.
b : Can't blame them. | Ice milk. Lovely. | Hot water and patience. | Ash on them. Somebody should.
a@slip : Give me your arm, then.
a : Poor things. | Not lovely. | No patience left. | Somebody, aye.

# ================================================================ Who's speaking

scene m7_wx_pup_questions
when topic=weather a.stage=young
a : Where does rain come from? | Why is snow cold? | Is the wind a wolf? | Does the sun get tired?
b[old] #tale : My grandmother said the sky wolves shake their coats. That's rain.
b : The sky. | Because it's snow. | No. It's air. Being rude. | Every evening. Same as us.
a@tale : That's silly. Is it true?
a : Why? | That's not an answer. | Rude air. | Then it goes to bed?
b?@tale : Truer than most things.

scene m7_wx_old_complaint
when topic=weather a.stage=old
a : I don't like the look of that sky. | Cold's in my bones today. | Weather's gone wrong. It's all wrong this year. | My knees are forecasting.
b[young] #pup : Why are you cross with the weather? It's just weather.
b : When do you like the look of a sky? | Sit by the fire. | It's always wrong, to you. | What do they say?
a@pup : You'll see. When you're my age, you'll see.
a : When it's over. | I will. | Because it is. | Worse by tomorrow. Mark me.

scene m7_wx_lovers_rain
when topic=weather pair.band=spouses here.weather=rain|snow|storm
a : Come here, you're soaked. | Share my cloak. | You'll catch your death. | Your paws are ice, #pet#.
b : Fuss. | It's not big enough for two. | Not today. | Warm them, then.
a : Somebody has to. | It is if we're close. | Tomorrow, then? | Gladly.

scene m7_wx_strangers_polite
when topic=weather pair.band=strangers
a : #greet#. Weather, eh? | Not a day to be out. | Could be worse, at least. | Bit of a #wind#.
b : Indeed. | No. | Could be. | A bit.
a : Well. | #farewell#. | Good day, then. | Mind how you go.
